[size=4]A dark theme for Mod Organizer 2 in the colours of my personal OC and upcoming VTuber model: near-black, bone-white and blood red, with red kept for what you've selected and the button you press most. Written from scratch, with its own icons.[/size]

[img]{IMG:strip-how-it-looks}[/img]

[list]
[*][b]Calm and dark.[/b] Near-black backgrounds, bone-white text and silver-outlined buttons, so a long mod list stays easy to scan.
[*][b]Red means something.[/b] It marks only what you've selected and the Run button, nothing else.
[*][b]Selections you can't lose.[/b] The selected row gets a thin bone outline as well as its fill, so it stays clear next to MO2's own conflict highlights.
[*][b]Easy to read.[/b] Every text colour is checked to reach 4.5:1 contrast or better on every background it can sit on.
[/list]

[img]{IMG:mod-list}[/img]
[size=2][i][color=#8a7f80]The mod list: bone text on near-black rows; Lighting Overhaul, the selected row, is outlined in bone beside MO2's own green and red conflict highlights.[/color][/i][/size]

[img]{IMG:strip-what-you-get}[/img]

[list]
[*][b]A big, branded Run button.[/b] Run and the program picker beside it are large and level, because you use them more than anything else. Run carries the sayngwyn deer skull and "RUN" on a dark red glow.
[*][b]New icons throughout.[/b] Simple line icons drawn for this theme: the toolbar, the buttons above the lists, check boxes, radio buttons and arrows. The problems icon is the only one in warning pink.
[*][b]Quiet details.[/b] Slim scrollbars, raised menus and tooltips, and tabs that turn red when selected.
[*][b]MO2's own meanings left alone.[/b] Conflict flags, plugin flags and the highlight colours you set in MO2 look exactly as MO2 draws them.
[/list]

[img]{IMG:run-area}[/img]
[size=2][i][color=#8a7f80]The Run area: a big Run button with the sayngwyn skull, the program picker beside it, and the active tab in red.[/color][/i][/size]

[img]{IMG:toolbar}[/img]
[size=2][i][color=#8a7f80]The toolbar: simple line icons drawn for this theme, with the menu bar above.[/color][/i][/size]

[img]{IMG:strip-install}[/img]

[list=1]
[*]Close Mod Organizer 2.
[*]From the download, copy the [b]stylesheets[/b] folder into your MO2 folder (the one with ModOrganizer.exe). Say yes if Windows asks to merge it with the existing stylesheets folder.
[*]Start MO2, open [b]Settings > Theme[/b], pick [b]sayngwyn[/b] and press OK.
[*]Restart MO2 once, so the toolbar picks up the new icons.
[/list]
Made for Mod Organizer 2.5.2, tested with Skyrim Special Edition and Fallout 4. Don't install it as a mod from MO2's Downloads tab: themes go in MO2's own stylesheets folder.

[img]{IMG:strip-good-to-know}[/img]

[list]
[*][b]Restart after picking it.[/b] MO2 only loads toolbar icons when it starts, so the toolbar changes after a restart.
[*][b]The title bar follows Windows.[/b] If it stays white, set Windows to dark mode for apps: Settings > Personalization > Colors > "Choose your default app mode: Dark".
[*][b]Links follow your Windows accent colour.[/b] No MO2 theme can change link colours. With Windows' default blue they're a little dim on the dark background.
[*][b]Conflict highlight colours are yours.[/b] MO2 keeps them in Settings > Theme > Colors; the theme doesn't change them.
[*][b]The Run button always says "RUN" in English,[/b] because the word is part of its picture.
[*][b]Programs pinned to the toolbar keep their own icons.[/b] MO2 doesn't let themes replace them.
[/list]

{BLOCK:MORE_BY}
[img]https://sayngwyn.com/img/page/divider-v4.png[/img]

[b]Made with AI.[/b] Claude contributed heavily under my direction: I decided what it does and how it looks and checked it, and the AI wrote most of the stylesheet, its icons and tests. It was checked live in a real Mod Organizer 2 with Skyrim Special Edition and Fallout 4 before release.

